\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that a Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and is such that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p,w)\equiv a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p,w)\equiv d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=p_{1}x_{1}(p,w)+p_{2}x_{2}(p,w)+p_{3}x_{3}(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since we have the expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

, we can solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 using the equation above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}(p,w)=\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}x_{1}(p,w)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}x_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\ \ =\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Prove 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=a+b\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+c\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}=a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}=x_{1}(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=d+e\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+f\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}=d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}=x_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=\frac{\alpha w}{\alpha p_{3}}-\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+c\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\right)-\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+f\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\ \ \ =\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)=x_{3}(p,w)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero.
\end_layout

